  {
    "episode_id": 81271,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81271.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "So I've done all of it. But now when I put, and you never say never, but now when I put a wig on, it just doesn't feel an alignment with where I'm at and who I am. It feels like a costume. And I feel resentful that I should have to put on one more piece of armor in order to safely negotiate the world and to navigate spaces. And I do want to give a shout out to the disability community and the alopecia community who enthusiastically and warmly embraced me right away. It is an incredible community and it goes through a lot. People think it's just hair, but a lot of people living with alopecia battle, social anxiety and depression. You have people coming up to you with all types of concoctions and prescriptions every day about what really ails you and not understanding that it is an autoimmune disease. So I'm making progress every day.",
